Critical findings:

1. **`bezierVertex` in p5 v2**: The v2 syntax is `bezierVertex(x, y)` — single point, multiple calls (multiples of bezierOrder default 3). The old 6-arg `bezierVertex(x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3)` is v1 syntax. The custom-shapes/exercise-3 uses 6-arg bezierVertex in the solution: `bezierVertex(100, 50, 300, 50, 300, 200)`. In v2, a 6-arg call... the syntax `bezierVertex(x, y, [u], [v])` only accepts 2-4 args or 3-5 args. A 6-arg call would be invalid — p5 might throw an error or ignore. So the custom-shapes/exercise-3 solution is BROKEN under p5 v2. This needs to be rewritten using v2 semantics (bezierOrder(3) + 3 calls per curve, or use vertex calls). Wait, but the task instructions say "Use bezierVertex(x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3) — it takes 6 args". That's teaching v1 syntax. Under v2 this is wrong.

Hmm wait — but the app bundles "latest" p5 (currently 2.3.2). So all course code must be v2-compatible. The curves.yaml and custom-shapes.yaml were written for v2 (spline, splineVertex). But custom-shapes/exercise-3 uses old 6-arg bezierVertex. Inconsistent.

Actually — wait, I need to double check whether v2 still accepts the 6-arg form for backward compat. The v2 reference lists only `bezierVertex(x, y, [u], [v])` and `bezierVertex(x, y, z, [u], [v])`. And the compatibility library "shapes.js" restores v1 behavior (6-arg). So plain p5 v2.3.2 does NOT support 6-arg bezierVertex — it will likely error. So custom-shapes/exercise-3 must be rewritten for v2. E.g.:
```
beginShape();
vertex(100, 200);
bezierVertex(100, 50);  // control point 1
bezierVertex(300, 50);  // control point 2
bezierVertex(300, 200); // endpoint
...
```
But with bezierOrder default 3, you need multiples of 3 bezierVertex calls per curve. Hmm, this complicates the exercise content. The task validation requires `bezierVertex exactArgs 6`. That whole exercise needs to be reworked for v2 semantics. This is a significant content fix.

Actually wait — I should verify by checking p5 v2.3.2 actual behavior of bezierVertex with 6 args. The reference says syntax only supports 2/3/4/5 args. If you pass 6, p5 v2 would treat it as... probably the first two params and ignore the rest, or throw. Either way the shape wouldn't render as intended (the blob). So the exercise is broken in v2.

2. **`loadImage` in p5 v2**: returns a Promise. The io/exercise-2 solution:
```
let img;
function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  img = loadImage("https://picsum.photos/200/200");
}
function draw() {
  background(240);
  if (img) {
    image(img, 100, 100, 200, 200);
  }
